# len of a string bounded by a validator: its length is an interval the checker does not know, and an interval that contains the target bounds refutes nothing.
-- source.cue --
import "strings"

s!: string & strings.MaxRunes(5)
-- target.cue --
n!: int & >=0 & <=20
-- lens.cue --
input: _
result: {n: len(input.s)}
-- out --
unknown
result.n is an integer computed by len(input.s), in [0, 9223372036854775807], which the checker cannot show within int64 and the target's uint & <=20 (the bounds contain every value the expression takes, but it need not take them, so they do not refute either); bound the fields it reads (int32, >=0 & <=100) (rule unknown-bounds) @ lens.cue:2:17
